The Women’s Assembly of the Confederation of Public Employees’ Trade Unions (KESK) held a press conference at its headquarters to mark November 25, the International Day for the Elimination of Violence Against Women. Döne Gevher, KESK’s Women’s Secretary, called on women to unite in collective struggle under the slogan: “We are not safe at home, on the streets or in the workplace. Let’s organise and strengthen the struggle.”


Stating that they would continue their fight against violence targeting women’s right to life, labour, bodies, identities and freedom, Döne Gevher said solidarity must be strengthened against wars, racism, anti-women policies and hate speech. She also reiterated the demand for justice for women who have lost their lives, particularly Gülistan Doku, Rojin Kabaiş and Şule Çet.


Violence against public sector workers


Döne Gevher also shared the findings of a survey conducted among women public sector employees concerning workplace violence. According to the survey, the women who participated reported experiencing:


-2.7% physical violence;


-21.8% verbal attacks;


-16.6% workplace bullying (mobbing);


-3.9% sexual harassment;


-18.1% discrimination.


The KESK Women’s Assembly called for femicides and suspicious deaths of women to be effectively investigated, for Law No. 6284 on the Protection of the Family and Prevention of Violence Against Women to be implemented, and for Turkey to rejoin the Istanbul Convention. Its demands also included the ratification of ILO Convention No. 190 and the establishment of independent mechanisms to address harassment and workplace bullying.


The statement further called for an end to insecure and flexible forms of employment, wage inequality and the gendered division of labour. It demanded that care services be made publicly provided and free of charge, that public childcare centres be expanded, and that employment policies be developed to eradicate women's poverty.


The KESK Women’s Assembly also called for the establishment of a Ministry of Women, guarantees for trade union rights, and the implementation of measures necessary to build a democratic, secular and peaceful society in which women can live freely and equally. The statement concluded with a call to strengthen women's collective and organised struggle.
